# Flutter — StatelessWidget, StatefulWidget and setState()

## 1. StatelessWidget

A `StatelessWidget` is a widget whose UI does **not have changing internal state**.

Example:

```dart id="h2k9qa"
class HomeScreen extends StatelessWidget {
  const HomeScreen({super.key});

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return Scaffold(
      body: Center(
        child: Text("Hello Flutter"),
      ),
    );
  }
}
```

The UI is based on the values you give it.

Use `StatelessWidget` when the widget doesn't need to change its own data.

Examples:

* Static text
* Logo
* Simple UI section
* Fixed card
* Reusable display widget

---

# 2. StatefulWidget

A `StatefulWidget` is used when the UI needs to **change while the app is running**.

Examples:

* Counter
* Checkbox
* Switch
* Text that changes
* Selected dropdown item
* Login form state
* Loading indicator
* Show/hide password

Basic structure:

```dart id="9p6k2d"
class CounterScreen extends StatefulWidget {
  const CounterScreen({super.key});

  @override
  State<CounterScreen> createState() => _CounterScreenState();
}

class _CounterScreenState extends State<CounterScreen> {

  int count = 0;

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return Scaffold(
      body: Center(
        child: Text("$count"),
      ),
    );
  }
}
```

---

# 3. Why are there two classes?

A StatefulWidget normally has:

```text id="4rj8tz"
StatefulWidget
      ↓
createState()
      ↓
State class
      ↓
build()
```

For example:

```dart id="1r8h0a"
class CounterScreen extends StatefulWidget {
```

This describes the widget.

And:

```dart id="3zq5vs"
class _CounterScreenState extends State<CounterScreen> {
```

This stores the **changing data**.

For beginners, remember:

```text
StatefulWidget
→ Widget definition

State class
→ Changing data + UI
```

---

# 4. What is State?

**State = data that can change while the app is running.**

Example:

```dart id="x8v4mn"
int count = 0;
```

Initially:

```text
count = 0
```

After clicking a button:

```text
count = 1
```

Then:

```text
count = 2
```

The value is changing, so it is **state**.

Other examples:

```dart id="7f2q5k"
bool isDark = false;

bool isLoading = false;

String selectedCountry = "India";

bool isPasswordVisible = false;
```

All of these can be state.

---

# 5. What is setState()?

`setState()` tells Flutter:

> "My data changed. Rebuild this UI."

Example:

```dart id="f8k3pv"
setState(() {
  count++;
});
```

The important idea is:

```text
Change data
    ↓
setState()
    ↓
Flutter rebuilds UI
    ↓
New value appears
```

---

# 6. Complete Counter Example

```dart id="q5w9ax"
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

class CounterScreen extends StatefulWidget {
  const CounterScreen({super.key});

  @override
  State<CounterScreen> createState() => _CounterScreenState();
}

class _CounterScreenState extends State<CounterScreen> {

  int count = 0;

  void increaseCount() {
    setState(() {
      count++;
    });
  }

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return Scaffold(
      appBar: AppBar(
        title: Text("Counter"),
      ),

      body: Center(
        child: Text(
          "$count",
          style: TextStyle(fontSize: 40),
        ),
      ),

      floatingActionButton: FloatingActionButton(
        onPressed: increaseCount,
        child: Icon(Icons.add),
      ),
    );
  }
}
```

When the button is clicked:

```text
count = 0
   ↓
button
   ↓
count++
   ↓
setState()
   ↓
build() runs again
   ↓
count = 1
```

---

# 7. Why can't we just change the variable?

You might think:

```dart id="2o5j7h"
count++;
```

Why do we need:

```dart id="g7t3cx"
setState(() {
  count++;
});
```

Because Flutter needs to know that the value changed.

Without `setState()`, Flutter may not rebuild the relevant UI.

So:

```text
count++
→ Data changes

setState()
→ Tell Flutter to update UI
```

---

# 8. Example with Switch

You already learned Switch:

```dart id="x1c8nm"
bool notifications = false;
```

When changed:

```dart id="b3y6fd"
Switch(
  value: notifications,

  onChanged: (value) {
    setState(() {
      notifications = value;
    });
  },
)
```

Here:

```text
Switch changes
      ↓
notifications changes
      ↓
setState()
      ↓
UI updates
```

This is why controls such as:

* Checkbox
* Radio
* Switch
* Dropdown

are commonly used inside a `StatefulWidget`.

---

# 9. Stateless vs Stateful

| StatelessWidget                  | StatefulWidget            |
| -------------------------------- | ------------------------- |
| UI doesn't manage changing state | UI manages changing state |
| Simpler                          | More structure            |
| No `setState()`                  | Can use `setState()`      |
| Static display                   | Interactive/dynamic UI    |
| Example: logo                    | Example: counter          |

### Easy memory

```text
Nothing changes
      ↓
StatelessWidget

Something changes
      ↓
StatefulWidget
      ↓
setState()
```

---

# 10. Very Important Rule

`setState()` should normally be called **inside the State class**:

```dart id="z9v5kx"
class _MyScreenState extends State<MyScreen> {

  bool isLoading = false;

  void changeLoading() {
    setState(() {
      isLoading = true;
    });
  }
}
```

You don't need `setState()` just because you changed a normal local variable.

You use it when the changed value should cause the widget's UI to rebuild.

---

# 11. Real-world examples

### Login

```text
isLoading = true
      ↓
Show CircularProgressIndicator
```

### Password

```text
isPasswordVisible = true
      ↓
Show password
```

### Cart

```text
cartCount = 3
      ↓
Cart icon shows 3
```

### Currency Converter

```text
selectedCurrency = "USD"
      ↓
UI shows USD
```

### Dark mode

```text
isDarkMode = true
      ↓
UI changes theme
```

These are all examples of **state changing the UI**.

---

## Remember this flow

```text
USER ACTION
    ↓
DATA CHANGES
    ↓
setState()
    ↓
build() runs
    ↓
UI updates
```

This is one of the most important Flutter concepts to understand before moving into more advanced topics.
